  { "q": "paste",            "want": "core:miso-paste", "ui": "en",
    "why": "Guards the tier order from one side: an English word, asked in the English app. In a FRENCH app it correctly returns Pasteque, whose own visible name begins with these five letters - measured identical to HEAD in all eleven, so the earlier all-language form of this probe was asserting something untrue." },
  { "q": "Brot",             "want": "core:pita", "ui": "de",
    "why": "Guards it from the other side, and only bites in a German ui. Four bean-sprout rows once outranked every bread here, reaching `starts` through the Spanish and Portuguese for sprouts - languages the typist is not reading." },

  { "q": "rice",             "want": "core:rice-white",
    "open": "A name that BEGINS with the word outranks one that merely contains it, by design - Rice noodles begins with it, White rice does not. Whether a bare ingredient word should instead mean the plainest food is undecided; it is the same question as potato vs sweet potato." },
  { "q": "Reis",             "want": "core:rice-white", "ui": "de",
    "open": "As rice: Reisnudeln begins with it, Weisser Reis does not." },
  { "q": "بطاطس",            "want": "core:potato-baked",
    "open": "Both Arabic names carry the word inside a parenthetical - بطاطا (بطاطس) مشوية / مسلوقة - so neither BEGINS with it and the tie is settled elsewhere than in the ten languages whose names lead with it. Baked and boiled are equally plain; this records that Arabic lands on the other one, not that either is wrong." },
  { "q": "carote",             "want": "core:carrot", "ui": "it",
